{
 "result": "csvsum.py is built and verified: it reads all of stdin as bytes, cuts the body at newline boundaries into up to 16 chunks (capped at the CPU count, 10 here), sums each chunk in a forked child, merges the per-cat sums in the parent, and prints `cat,sum` with 2 decimals sorted by cat. On the generated 2,000,000-row file (41 MB, 53 categories) the assay (1 / best-of-3 wall time, 0 if output differs from expected.txt) reads 8.5 against the registered single-process baseline of 1.71, i.e. about 0.58 s down to about 0.12 s. Output is byte-identical to expected.txt for both file stdin and piped stdin, and an independent reference using the csv module with Decimal sums also matches expected.txt. A small hand-made input and an empty input (exit 0) also behave correctly. Sums are accumulated as binary floats, so they agree with exact decimal sums on this data but that is not guaranteed for much larger magnitudes or row counts. Irregular lines fall back to a slower per-line path, and inputs under 1 MB or platforms without fork run single-process. numpy was not tried. The folder still holds leftover experiment copies (csvsum_base.py, csvsum_new.py, csvsum_par.py) that are not needed.",
 "calls": 12,
